WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.320
Hello everyone.

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:04.840
Welcome to the Langfocus channel and my name
is Paul.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:09.780
Today we'll be answering the question “How
are British English and American English Different?”

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.990
- one of the most commonly asked questions
by learners of English.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:17.619
And hopefully native speakers of English will
learn a thing or two from this video as well.

00:00:17.619 --> 00:00:22.160
The truth is that both British and American
English have numerous varieties, in other

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.689
words various accents and dialects, so in
(the main part of) this video I will try to

00:00:26.689 --> 00:00:30.860
focus on the most standard, non-regional variety
of each one.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:34.050
Disclaimer: I'm not American, I'm Canadian.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:38.800
But I'm confident that we will someday be
Americans after the invasion.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.490
Standard Canadian English is very very close
to General American English, so I will say

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:50.129
the American examples myself, unless there's
a need to distinguish American pronunciation

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:51.129
from Canadian.

00:00:51.129 --> 00:00:55.300
There are several ways in which Britain English
and the American English different: vocabulary,

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.789
accent, spelling, and grammar.

00:00:57.789 --> 00:01:01.320
Vocabulary

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.930
In the US, people generally say “garbash”
or “trash”, while in the UK they generally

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.440
say “rubbish”.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:08.910
Both literally and figuratively.

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:12.030
“The game was rubbish!”

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:15.390
Americans “go on vacation”, while Brits
“go on holidays”.

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.910
And this is also possible in American English.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:23.220
In the US people rent “apartments”, while
in the UK they rent “flats”.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:27.630
In the US, if your apartment is at street
level, then you live on the first floor, and

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.140
the person above you lives on the second floor.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:36.010
In the UK, you live on the ground floor, and
the person above you lives on the first floor.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:40.700
If that person above you is unable or just
too lazy to take the stairs, in the US they'd

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.220
take the elevator.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.250
In the UK, they'd take the lift.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:49.180
When you're bored at home, in the US you might
turn on the TV, while in the UK you would

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:51.460
turn on the telly.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:55.540
When you step outside of your building to
go for a walk, in the US you might walk on

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:59.880
the sidewalk, while in the UK you walk on
the pavement.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.550
And if you're tired of walking, in the US
you might take the subway.

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:06.350
In the UK, you take the underground.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:11.280
In the US, it's perfectly to wear pants when
you're riding the subway, but in the UK you'd

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:16.140
better wear some TROUSERS too because pants
means underpants.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:21.640
And specifically women's underpants are sometimes
called knickers in the UK.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:27.620
So when someone overreacts, in the US you
might say “Don't get your panties in a bunch!”

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:31.360
In the UK you'd say “Don't get your knickers
in a twist!”.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:32.980
Paul how dare you be so crude.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:36.000
Now I can't show this video to my 6 year old
students!

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.950
Don't worry, they'll watch it on their phones
during recess.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:43.830
Going back to the word “pants” for a moment,
it can also be used in British English as

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.780
an adjective, meaning something is “crappy”
or “it sucks”.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:50.240
For example “That album is pants”.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:54.450
In American English, you might say “That
album sucks”.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:55.450
Accent

00:02:55.450 --> 00:03:02.210
So I'll try to focus on General American English,
and for the UK - Received Pronunciation.

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:07.160
These are the accents you're likely to hear
on CNN and the BBC, respectively.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.160
R-sounds

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.130
American English is rhotic, meaning that “r”
sounds are always clearly pronounced.

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:17.450
British English is non-rhotic, meaning that
the “r” sound is not pronounced unless

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:19.290
it is followed by a vowel sound.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:20.290
Listen to the difference.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:21.830
US: “My father's in the car”.

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.530
UK: “My father's in the car”.

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:25.850
Now let's focus on two words.

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:27.230
US: father UK: father.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.450
US: car UK: car.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:32.920
Notice that the final r sound is not pronounced
in British English.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:35.770
“Father” ends in a simple schwa vowel
/ˈfɑː.ðə/.

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:41.020
And in “car” the a vowel sound is lengthened
in place of the “r” sound.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.020
/kɑː/

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:46.290
Now, the thing about British non-rhotic dialects
that I find pretty wild is something called

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:47.710
the intrusive r.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:52.590
That means that people sometimes add an r-sound
to a word that doesn't actually have one,

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:55.290
if it's followed by a vowel in the next word.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:57.780
For example, in the sentence “I saw a film”.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.630
In British English it sometimes sounds like
this: “I saw'r a film”.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:05.010
So you can hear that there's an “r” sound
connecting “saw” and “a”.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:10.340
I once had British on-the-job trainer, and
she said “Hello my name is Paula and I'll

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:11.710
be your trainer today”.

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:13.060
I remember thinking “Pauler?

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:14.840
What, you can't say your own name?”

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.229
But, it wasn't just her.

00:04:16.229 --> 00:04:18.130
That was the “intrusive r”.

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:19.130
T-sounds

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:23.470
In British English (and again, I must emphasize
that I'm talking about the accent referred

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:29.070
to as Received Pronunciation), t sounds are
pronounced as hard Ts, in other words voiceless

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.620
/t/ sounds.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:36.500
In the US, they sometimes sound like /ɾ/
(an alevelar tap) instead of /t/ (an alveolar

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:37.500
stop).

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:42.320
This normally occurs in an unstressed syllable,
between 2 vowel sounds, or between a vowel

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:45.230
and a rhotic sound (like an “r” sound).

00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:46.920
So in the US people say butter.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:47.920
[ˈbʌɾɚ].

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.590
And in the UK, they say butter.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:50.590
/ˈbʌ.tə/.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:52.320
In the US: Stop fighting! /stɑp ˈfʌɪɾɪŋ/.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.760
In the UK: Stop fighting! /stɒp ˈfʌɪtɪŋ/.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.940
You may have also noticed the “o” sound
in the word “stop” was a little different,

00:04:58.940 --> 00:04:59.950
which brings me to...

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:00.950
O sounds

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:05.630
In the word “stop”, the American “o”
sound is an unrounded vowel /ɑ/ while the

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:08.410
British “o” sound is rounded /ɒ/.

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:11.000
Another example: US hot /hɑt/ UK: /hɒt/.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.740
There is also the “o” diphthong in the
word “know” US /noʊ/ (US).

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:20.650
In the UK: /nəʊ/ . In the UK the sound is
a schwa followed by /ʊ/ as in “put”.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:24.280
US: show /ʃoʊ/ UK: show /ʃəʊ/

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:25.330
A sounds.

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:28.340
In other words, sounds represented by the
letter “a”)

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:33.080
/ɑː/ in UK normally becomes an /æ/ sound
in American English.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:35.420
For example, in the UK: half /hɑːf/.

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:38.530
And in the US: half /hæf/.

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:41.730
Words that are /æ/ in UK remain pretty similar
in US.

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:44.220
For example, in the UK: cat /kæt/.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:46.940
And in the US: /kæt/.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:51.840
An exception is a small set of words in which
the “a” is followed by “rr”, in which

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.240
case the vowel is pronounced as /e/.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:59.320
In the UK: marry /ˈmæɹɪ/ . In the US:
marry /ˈmɛɹi/.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:03.750
Because of the difference, in the US “marry”
and “Merry” sound the same.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:07.420
“Carry” and “Kerry” sound the same.

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:11.870
Spelling: American and British spellings are
largely the same, but there are a few notable

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:12.870
differences.

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:17.110
This is in large part because Noah Webster
(whom the Webster dictionary is named after)

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:21.840
made an effort to reform English spelling
in the 1700s, in order to make the words spelled

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:23.120
the way they sounded.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:26.050
This resulted in some spelling changes in
American English.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:31.350
Most (but not all) words that end in ~re in
the UK end in ~er in the US.

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:34.500
For example: centre/center, theatre/theater,
metre/meter, sombre/somber.

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:41.639
Some words that end in ~nce in the UK are
spelled with ~nse in the US.

00:06:41.639 --> 00:06:42.800
licence/license.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:45.120
Defence/defense, offence/offense.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:51.180
Some words with “ou” in the UK are spelled
with “o” in the US.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:54.290
Colour/color, favour/favor, honour/honor,
labour/labor, etc.

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:58.389
The ending ~ise became ~ize in the US.

00:06:58.389 --> 00:06:59.520
organise/organize.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:00.650
apologise/apologize.

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:05.440
A similar change also occurs in other contexts
where the “s” is voiced (in other words

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:07.930
it makes a /z/ sound).

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:08.980
Analyse/analyze.

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:10.030
Cosy/cozy.

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:13.570
There are verbs ending with “l” that take
a doubled “l” in British English when

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:15.110
a suffix is added.

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:17.540
In American English there is no double “l”.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:20.390
travelled/traveled,
cancelled/canceled, marvellous / marvelous.

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:23.960
If you're wondering how the last one fits
in with the others, remember that “marvel”

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:28.320
is a verb, and then an adjectival suffix is
added to it).

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:33.170
Grammar: There are only very minor differences
in grammar between British English and American

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:34.170
English.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:35.250
Auxiliary verbs.

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:39.750
Brits use “shall” for the future much
more than Americans, as well as to ask for

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.480
advice or an opinion.

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:43.470
Some difference in preposition use:

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:48.830
In the US, people say “on the weekend”,
but in the UK they say “at the weekend”.

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:52.790
And in the US, people say “different from”
or “different than”, but in the UK they

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:56.810
say “different from” or “different to”.

00:07:56.810 --> 00:07:59.000
There are some different past tense forms.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:03.270
For example, in American English the past
tense of the world “learn” is “learned”,

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:05.900
while in British English it's more common
to say “learnt”.

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:10.820
Actually, both forms are used in either country,
but there is more of tendency towards one

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:11.820
form.

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:18.790
This is true for other words like dreamed
vs. dreamt, burned vs. burnt, leaned vs. leant.

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:19.790
Another example.

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:23.300
In the US, the past tense of dive is usually
“dove”.

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:25.100
In the UK it's “dived”.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:29.380
Maybe the American form developed by analogy
with “drive” and “drove”.

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:33.580
Anyways, differences like these are not consistent,
but you'll notice some different past tense

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:35.789
forms here and there.

00:08:35.789 --> 00:08:39.460
Past participles: Sometimes past participles
have a different form.

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:42.370
The most well-known example is for the verb
“get”.

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:45.089
In the US, there's get / got / gotten.

00:08:45.089 --> 00:08:47.730
But in the UK, it's get / got / got/.

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:53.140
** Both forms have existed since the Middle
English period, but “gotten” has fallen

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:55.000
out of use in the UK.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:59.860
“Got” can be used in American English
in the form “have got”, but with the meaning

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:03.230
of “have”, not “has received/become”.

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:06.140
US: I haven't gotten the eviction notice yet.

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:08.520
UK: I haven't got the eviction notice yet.

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:13.100
Sentences – Alright, let's check a couple
of sentences and see what we find.

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:16.170
In the US: I think we need a lawyer.

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:19.440
In the UK: I reckon we need a solicitor.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:21.730
You'll notice that a couple of words are different.

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:26.190
British people often use the word “reckon”
which means “think” or “suppose”.

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:28.310
Americans know this word, but rarely use it.

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:33.170
And while Americans would typically refer
to a professional legal consultant as a lawyer,

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:37.550
in the UK they often say “solicitor” which
is a type of lawyer that does consultation.

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:42.310
The type of lawyer who represents you in court
in the UK in usually a barrister, while in

00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:46.190
the US they are usually referred to as attorneys.

00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:47.320
Another sentence.

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:50.320
In the US: I'm going for a beer with my friends.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:53.890
In the UK: I'm going for a pint with my mates.

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:57.800
Notice that British people often “pint”
where Americans would say “beer”.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:02.200
Brits also say beer as a countable noun like
this, but pint is used frequently.

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:06.950
And notice that Brits often say “mate”
where Americans would say “friend”.

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:11.070
The differences between British English and
American English might seem surprising or

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:15.980
amusing, but remember: in this video I'm zooming
in on the differences and focusing on them.

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:18.060
For the most part they are actually the same.

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:22.920
There are some minor differences in vocabulary,
in pronunciation, in grammar, and in spelling,

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:26.790
but any native speaker with a little bit of
exposure to the other will quickly adapt to

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:31.080
these differences and be able to understand
the other variety without any problem.

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:35.460
The differences are sometimes greater if we
focus on regional dialects and sociolects

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:37.790
of British English and American English.

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:42.020
While most Americans probably have no trouble
understanding Received Pronunciation, they

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:46.630
mayhave some trouble understanding Cockney
English, or the Georgie English of Northeastern

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:48.560
England, or other varieties.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:53.089
But as far as standard, non-regional speech
goes, I'd say that the differences are minimal.

00:10:53.089 --> 00:10:57.250
However, learners of English who focus on
one of the two varieties, will likely have

00:10:57.250 --> 00:11:01.930
a bit of trouble understanding the other until
they gain significant more exposure to it.

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:06.550
The QOD: What other differences between American
and British English are you aware of?

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:12.090
In this video I was only able to give a limited
number of examples, so add yours in the comments!

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:15.110
Be sure to follow Langfocus on Facebook, Twitter,
and Instagram.

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:19.050
And once again, thank you to all of my wonderful
